{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788201263,"nonce":"WkOEwFgWZJRgLnb8RSMzUoOsnrA=","title":"WorldSafe's zone-hazard note still says map-view trips it; Spatial.cs fixed that and the note now misdirects","message":"Found by W1 (autorimmer-27) while vetting f7b6207, session 9.\n\n`WorldSafe.cs:57-60` carries a note saying `map-view` trips the `Zone.Cells`\nhazard. That is stale — `Spatial.cs:243-244` was fixed and map-view now routes\nthrough the guarded accessor.\n\nSmall, but this repo's hazard notes are load-bearing: they are what a worker\nreads before touching an observer, and this workspace has already been bitten\ntwice by stale notes read as authoritative (see the Factions build-debt bullet\nin the workspace CLAUDE.md, where two separate review agents were misled by a\nstale claim). A note naming a caller that no longer has the problem sends the\nnext reader to audit the wrong file, and — worse — implies the hazard is\nhandled somewhere it is not.\n\nThe underlying hazards are real and must stay documented: `Zone.Cells` runs a\nFisher-Yates shuffle off the shared RNG and permanently reorders a scribed\nlist; `zg.GetPlantDefToGrow()` assigns and SCRIBES a default onto a\nnever-configured zone. Both Class R. `WorldSafe.ZoneCells` and\n`WorldSafe.PlantToGrow` remain the guarded routes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The note names the hazard and the guarded route without naming a caller that\n  no longer trips it.\n- If any caller DOES still trip it, that caller is named instead — verified by\n  grep at the time of the fix, not assumed.","files":null}]}